// =============================================================================
// ui/tsconfig.node.json
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Purpose:
//   TypeScript project for the Node-executed half of the ui tree: exactly two
//   files, vite.config.ts and vitest.config.ts. Vite and Vitest load those two
//   at start-up, in Node, before any browser bundle exists -- so they are the
//   only TypeScript in this tree that may legitimately reach for a Node API.
//   This project type-checks them (it never emits) and grants them the Node
//   type definitions that ui/tsconfig.json deliberately withholds from the
//   browser sources under ui/src.
//
//   Two callers consume it: an explicit `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.node.json`
//   check, and ui/eslint.config.js, which lists this project alongside
//   ui/tsconfig.json so both config files are linted WITH type information
//   rather than falling out of the lint gate entirely.
//
// WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
//   - Alternatives Considered: one combined tsconfig spanning ui/src and these
//     two files was evaluated and rejected. A single project has a single
//     `types` list, so it would have to either add "node" -- putting `process`,
//     `__dirname`, `Buffer` and `require` in scope for all 21 React screens,
//     where `process.env.API_URL` inside a component would type-check cleanly
//     and then throw at run time in a browser that has no `process` -- or omit
//     "node" and leave these two files unable to type-check at all. Two
//     sibling projects are the only arrangement that types both halves without
//     lending Node globals to browser code.
//   - Alternatives Considered: the stock-Vite layout, in which the root
//     tsconfig carries only `references` and this project sets
//     `composite: true`, was rejected because here it is silently inert.
//     `tsc -p` does not traverse project references; only `tsc -b` does, and
//     nothing in this tree invokes `-b`. All three consumers of the browser
//     project run the literal `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`
//     (ui/package.json's `typecheck` and `build` scripts, and the type-check
//     gate in .github/workflows/ui-ci.yml). Under a references-only root that
//     owns no files of its own, each of those three commands would check ZERO
//     files and still exit 0 -- a gate that reports success having verified
//     nothing. The two projects are therefore siblings, each reached by its own
//     invocation, and neither references the other.
//   - Assumption: the compiler is typescript 6.0.3 as pinned in
//     ui/package.json, and it stays there. typescript-eslint 8.65.0
//     peer-declares `typescript >=4.8.4 <6.1.0`; raising the compiler past that
//     ceiling stops the type-aware lint pass -- and with it the JSDoc
//     documentation gate -- while the build stays green, so the loss is
//     invisible to whoever raised it.
// =============================================================================
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    // WHY (Assumption): ES2022 is the language level of the Node line this
    // tree is validated on -- ui/package.json requires node >=22.12.0 and the
    // UI build stage runs node:22.23.1-alpine. No older Node runtime has to be
    // supported, and nothing here is shipped to a browser, so the
    // down-levelling that a lower target would cost buys nothing back.
    "target": "ES2022",

    // WHY (Trade-off): the library set is ES2022 alone -- no browser library is
    // listed. These files run in Node before any browser exists, so admitting
    // DOM types would let `document` or `window` appear in a config file and
    // type-check, only to fail the moment Vite loaded it -- the mirror image of
    // the leak the two-project split prevents. The compromise accepted is that
    // a config file cannot share a DOM-typed helper with ui/src, which it has
    // no reason to do.
    "lib": ["ES2022"],

    // WHY (Assumption): ui/package.json declares "type": "module", so both
    // files are ESM. Resolution is "bundler" rather than "nodenext" because
    // Vite and Vitest never hand these files to Node's own resolver -- each is
    // bundled with esbuild first and the result executed, so specifiers that
    // Node's ESM resolver would reject do resolve at load time. Matching the
    // checker to the actual loader keeps tsc from reporting import failures
    // that cannot happen in practice.
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",

    // WHY (Assumption): Node executes these two files, so the APIs they reach
    // for -- resolving a path against the project root, reading the process
    // environment -- genuinely exist for them. @types/node 26.1.1 supplies the
    // declarations, pinned in ui/package.json. The counterpart matters just as
    // much: ui/tsconfig.json leaves this list without "node", and that absence
    // is what forces browser code to read configuration through Vite's
    // `import.meta.env` instead of `process.env`.
    "types": ["node"],

    // WHY (Assumption): Vite and Vitest transpile these files through their
    // own loaders, so no consumer anywhere reads a tsc output. Emitting would
    // leave a JavaScript tree beside the sources that nothing imports and that
    // the root .gitignore does not name -- untracked clutter bought for
    // nothing. The compiler's whole role here is to check.
    "noEmit": true,

    // WHY (Trade-off): full strictness is applied to configuration code, not
    // only to application code. The ceremony costs little across two short
    // files, and it buys something specific: a misspelled or wrongly-typed
    // Vite or Vitest option is caught by this check, rather than surfacing
    // later as a build that quietly omitted the React plugin or a test run
    // whose jsdom environment and setup file were never applied. Both of those
    // failures present a long way from their cause.
    "strict": true,

    // WHY (Trade-off): the four checks below are not implied by `strict`. In
    // files whose entire job is to assemble an options object, an unused local
    // or a case that falls through is almost always an edit left half-finished,
    // so failing on one is more useful than tolerating it.
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,

    // WHY (Assumption): esbuild -- which is what actually transforms these
    // files under Vite and Vitest -- compiles one file at a time and cannot
    // look across a module boundary to tell whether an imported name is a type
    // or a value. These two flags make tsc reject precisely the constructs
    // esbuild would therefore mishandle, so a type-only import has to say
    // `import type` here instead of being erased by luck.
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,

    // WHY (Trade-off): declaration files shipped by dependencies are not
    // re-checked. The alternative spends the check re-validating third-party
    // .d.ts files this project cannot edit, where a conflict inside a
    // transitive dependency of the Vite or Vitest chain would block a check
    // whose real subject is two local files.
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    // WHY (Assumption): parts of the Vite and Vitest dependency chain are
    // still published as CommonJS, so a default import of one has to be
    // interoperable for these files to consume it the way the loader will.
    "esModuleInterop": true,

    // WHY (Assumption): the CI runner and the container build stage use a
    // case-sensitive filesystem, so a specifier whose casing differs from the
    // file on disk has to fail here -- on a developer's case-insensitive
    // machine it would otherwise resolve locally and break only in CI.
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,

    // WHY (Assumption): a build config legitimately reads declarative data --
    // deriving a value from ui/package.json, for instance -- and typing that
    // import from the file's real shape is what keeps it from drifting away
    // from a hand-written declaration of the same data.
    "resolveJsonModule": true,

    // WHY (Assumption): every file here is a module, so each is treated as one
    // unconditionally instead of being classified by whether it happens to
    // carry a top-level import or export. Left to that inference, a config file
    // that exported nothing would be read as a global script and its
    // declarations would land in the global scope.
    "moduleDetection": "force"
  },

  // WHY (Assumption): the two files are named literally rather than matched by
  // a glob such as `*.ts`. This project is the one place in the ui tree that
  // hands out Node types, so its membership has to stay a deliberate list:
  // under a glob the next root-level .ts file anyone adds would join it
  // silently and inherit `process` and `Buffer` without that ever being
  // chosen. Widening it should require editing this line. No `exclude`
  // accompanies it -- a two-entry list is already closed, and a second bound
  // would only raise the question of which one wins.
  "include": ["vite.config.ts", "vitest.config.ts"]
}
